    {
      "entryId": "ramcharitmanas-bala-kanda-0170",
      "sequence": 182,
      "meaning": "The king obeys and sleeps, while the deceiver sits plotting. Because Pratāpabhānu is exhausted, sleep comes readily, though anxiety has kept him awake. The demon Kālaketu arrives—the very boar who misled the king. A close friend of the false ascetic, he is master of immense deceit. Pratāpabhānu had earlier slain Kālaketu’s hundred sons and ten brothers in battle because they tormented Brahmins, saints and gods; recalling that old feud, demon and false sage plan the enemy’s destruction, while the fated king knows nothing. Tulsīdāsa warns that even a small but powerful foe should not be dismissed: Rāhu’s severed head still afflicts sun and moon.",
